::GEO_U01_L06_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>In the statement If p, then q, what is p called? {
=The hypothesis #Correct. The hypothesis is the condition that follows if.
~The conclusion #The conclusion is q, the result that follows then.
~The converse #The converse is a related statement, not p itself.
~The contrapositive #The contrapositive is a related statement, not p itself.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the converse of If p, then q? {
~If not p, then not q #This is the inverse, not the converse.
=If q, then p #Correct. The converse switches the hypothesis and conclusion.
~If not q, then not p #This is the contrapositive, not the converse.
~p if and only if q #This is a biconditional form, not only the converse.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the inverse of If p, then q? {
=If not p, then not q #Correct. The inverse keeps the order and negates both parts.
~If q, then p #This is the converse, not the inverse.
~If not q, then not p #This is the contrapositive, not the inverse.
~If p, then q #This is the original conditional, not the inverse.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the contrapositive of If p, then q? {
~If q, then p #This is the converse, not the contrapositive.
~If not p, then not q #This is the inverse, not the contrapositive.
=If not q, then not p #Correct. The contrapositive switches and negates both parts.
~If p, then not q #This changes only the conclusion and is not the contrapositive.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>What does the phrase if and only if mean in a biconditional statement? {
~Only the original conditional is being stated #A biconditional includes both directions, not only one direction.
~Only the converse is being stated #A biconditional includes both directions, not only the converse.
=Both the conditional and its converse are being stated #Correct. If and only if means the relationship works in both directions.
~Both parts of the statement are negated #Negating both parts describes inverse language, not biconditional language.
}
